# labeldrift

Find hardcoded label/enum strings that have drifted from their canonical
constant definitions.

Most codebases of any size define a module of canonical string constants
(`STATUS_ACTIVE = "ACTIVE"`, `PRIORITY_HIGH = "HIGH"`, ...) and then, over
time, someone writes `"ACTIVE"` or `"HIGH"` directly instead of importing the
constant. The literal still works at runtime, so nothing breaks — until the
canonical value changes and the bare string doesn't, or a typo (`"ACTVE"`)
slips through with no error. `labeldrift` catches the drift before it ships.

It is config-driven and AST-based: it doesn't need to import your code, so
it works even on packages with heavy runtime dependencies, and it never
produces false positives from comments (only exact string-literal matches).

## Install

```bash
pip install labeldrift
```

## Quickstart

Add a `labeldrift.toml` next to your `pyproject.toml`:

```toml
[source]
module = "myproject.labels"   # used only to make messages actionable

[[label]]
value = "CRITICAL"
constant = "PRIORITY_CRITICAL"

[[label]]
value = "HIGH"
constant = "PRIORITY_HIGH"
# Ambiguous single words are common across unrelated domains — scope
# them to the paths where they're actually a label, not just English:
context = ["myproject/billing", "myproject/alerts"]
```

Then run:

```bash
labeldrift check src/
```

```
✗ Found 2 label drift violation(s) in 1 file(s):

  src/myproject/billing/invoice.py:42: use myproject.labels.PRIORITY_CRITICAL instead of bare string 'CRITICAL'
  src/myproject/alerts/rules.py:17: use myproject.labels.PRIORITY_HIGH instead of bare string 'HIGH'
```

Exit code is `0` when clean, `1` when violations are found, `2` on
configuration/usage errors — drop it straight into CI.

## Config

Config can live in either place (checked in this order):

1. An explicit file: `labeldrift check src/ --config path/to/labeldrift.toml`
2. `./labeldrift.toml`
3. A `[tool.labeldrift]` table inside `./pyproject.toml`

| Key | Required | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `source.module` | no | Dotted module path shown in violation messages |
| `[[label]]` | yes (at least one) | One rule per literal to detect |
| `label.value` | yes | The exact string literal to flag |
| `label.constant` | yes | The canonical constant name to suggest instead |
| `label.context` | no | List of path substrings; if set, only flag inside paths containing one of them. Omit to flag everywhere. |
| `exclude.paths` | no | Path substrings to skip entirely (defaults to `.venv`, `__pycache__`, `node_modules`, `.git`) |

The `context` field is the key design point: a value like `"HIGH"` is too
common an English word to flag globally without noise, but inside
`myproject/alerts/` it unambiguously means a priority level. Scope each rule
to where it's actually a label.

## Pre-commit

```yaml
- repo: https://github.com/mjlak1000/labeldrift
  rev: v0.1.0
  hooks:
    - id: labeldrift
```

## CI (GitHub Actions)

```yaml
- name: Check label drift
  run: pip install labeldrift && labeldrift check src/
```

## Why not just use pylint's magic-value check?

Generic magic-value linters flag *any* unnamed literal — useful, but noisy,
and they don't know which literals are actually your project's enum values
versus ordinary strings. `labeldrift` is narrower on purpose: you tell it
exactly which values matter and where, so every hit is a real, actionable
fix back to a named constant.

## Development

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest
ruff check src/ tests/
```
